<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.8.4: Determining if an Expression Can Be Rewritten in Factored Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02209</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.8.4: Determining if an Expression Can Be Rewritten in Factored Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1193ae77-ba1c-42c3-87b8-3406595201b8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Write each expression in factored form. If it is not possible, write “not possible.”</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(a^2− 36\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((a + 6)(a − 6)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(49 − 25b^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((7 + 5b)(7 − 5b)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(c^2+ 9\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Not possible because this is the sum of two squares.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(\frac {100}{81}-16d^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((\frac {10}{9} + 4d)(\frac {10}{9} − 4d)\)</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Press Pause</title>
<para>If students are still struggling to identify and factor special polynomials, have them access the practice problems and additional resources from the “Factor Special Products” lesson in Unit 6. </para>
</section>
</content>
</document>
